  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    The period following the retreat of the great ice sheets, during which penguin civilization expanded from its established holdings at the Aurora Stack and the Nightfall Shelf into newly accessible waters rich with krill and with secrets frozen since before any penguin memory.
    
    Explorers from both colonies founded settlements across the revealed territory. ☽'scar Ramtatus~ was established by Nightshelf settlers who believed the colony could become more than a mining outpost. The krill-bloom narratives that dominate popular accounts overstate the ease of expansion. The Foundation strata preserve impressions of expeditions that ended in starvation, of rookeries founded and abandoned within a season. The failures outnumber the successes by a margin I find difficult to quantify, because the ice remembers the dead more reliably than it remembers the merely disappointed.
    
    Guild halls rose in every settlement, their charters pressed into sheets of ice — most since shattered or buried. The Flipper Accord formalized trade between the two colonies: aurora-crystals for fire-cores. The public version suggests a single negotiation between willing partners. The Foundation Depths contain three earlier drafts with substantively different terms, frozen in administrative ice at intervals suggesting months of disputed revision. The final text held because neither colony had yet tested whether the other could be coerced rather than courted.
    
    Notable creations of this era include the Compass of Crystal, crafted by the artisan Selka; the Crystalline Lineage, an aurora-inscribed genealogical record of Aurora Stack's founding families; and the E'frostlings trading company, built by Assessor Zheishongfeihua.
    
    Orca pods patrolled the deep currents throughout — distant shapes reported by scouts, stories traded at the ice-edge. The standard account frames this as background threat, raids sporadic and almost ignorable. But penguin expansion into thawed waters was also expansion into established orca hunting grounds, a fact the era's optimism rendered invisible. The consequences would not arrive for generations, but they were already being earned.
    
    Every account of the Great Thaw written after the Faction Wars treats it as prologue. The era lasted long enough for its inhabitants to regard it as permanent, which is the only honest measure of how thoroughly it ended.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771595045294_0", anchor: "The consequences would not arrive for generations", text: "At least two raids later catalogued under the Orca Incursion correlate in location and timing with Great Thaw-era encroachment into documented orca calving waters. The deep ice at ☽'scar Ramtatus~ preserves thermal signatures consistent with orca shore-breach tactics decades before the Incursion's accepted start date. The consequences were not deferred. They were reclassified.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771595045294_1", anchor: "neither colony had yet tested whether the other could be coerced rather than courted", text: "The second draft's terms suggest otherwise. It allocates fire-core distribution rights exclusively through Aurora-appointed intermediaries — a structure indistinguishable from tributary arrangement. Someone tested. Someone refused.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771595045294_2", anchor: "crafted by the artisan Selka", text: "Selka appears in one ice-impression and zero administrative records. The attribution survives because Zheishongfeihua's restoration notes name a prior maker. The word may be a rank designation rather than a personal name — archaic Nightshelf usage assigns \"selka\" to crystal-cutters of a specific grade. I have left it as a name in the main text because the alternative is admitting the Compass of Crystal's creator is anonymous.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
